module gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, Int, String }
import std.logic { Bool }
import std.measure { ByteSize, byte_size, byte_size_count, BasisPoint, basis_point, basis_point_count }
import std.realization_width { int_min, int_max }
import std.disposition { Disposition, Terminal, Scaffold, SingleAuthority }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import std.dissolution { DissolutionCondition, DissolutionUnbound, DissolutionPending, DissolutionFired, unbound_dissolution, dissolution_status }
import gunbc.host.host_budget_source {
  HostBudgetResolution,
  HostBudgetResolved,
  HostBudgetDeclaredUnverified,
  HostBudgetUnreadable,
  HostBudgetExternallyBounded,
}

// WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS. extdeps.realization.reconcile_in_process declares the
// typed module cache's ceiling as RuntimeDerivedLimit { authority: ... }, and
// that authority pointed at v1_compiler.cli_run typed_module_cache_cap_derivation
// -- a hand-Rust symbol spelled inside a DeclarationRef. A DeclarationRef is
// structurally a reference to a MODULED declaration, so putting a seed symbol in
// one makes an unmodelled fact look resolved: nothing in the corpus states the
// division, the clamp, or the refusal, and no reader can check the seed against
// anything. RuntimeDerivedLimit is honest that the VALUE is computed at run
// start; it is not licence for the DERIVATION to be unmodelled.
//
// So the derivation lands here as the authority, and the seed keeps its own copy
// as a declared Scaffold below rather than as an implicit fork.
// The typed-module-cache entry cap, modelled. The cap bounds the memory used to RESOLVE the corpus,
// which is why the seed cannot simply read this value: cli_run enforce_typed_cache_entry_cap needs
// it while modules are being resolved, so it cannot be a value obtained by resolving them. That is
// a genuine bootstrap constraint rather than ordinary seed-shrink debt, and it is the reason the
// mirror below is a Scaffold with a stated dissolution rather than a deletion. What changes here is
// which side is the AUTHORITY: this module states the derivation, and the seed is checkable against
// it.
//
// The three constants are cited to the v1-run-stability M0/M1 receipts (~2-11 MiB per module
// observed; 3 MiB is the interim declared denominator until measured space lands) and the
// floor/ceiling the seed already enforces. The refusal arm is not a default: an unreadable budget
// yields CapEntryRefused, never the ceiling. That distinction is the whole receipt of
// gunbc.host.host_budget_source no_fallback_arm_note -- the previous unwrap_or(CEIL) turned an unknown
// budget into the most permissive cap available and OOM-killed the witness corpus twice.

data typed_module_bytes_per_entry_estimate: ByteSize = byte_size(count: 3145728)

data typed_module_cache_entries_floor: Int = 100

data typed_module_cache_entries_ceiling: Int = 4000

// THE WORKING-SET BOUND'S AUTHORITY IS THE CGROUP'S LIVE HEADROOM, NOT THE ENTRY COUNT.
//
// Earliest unjustified boundary (method: earliest_unjustified_boundary). The chain that thrashed:
// the cap bounds a byte budget by dividing it by `typed_module_bytes_per_entry_estimate` (3 MiB,
// itself a declared Scaffold denominator "until measured space lands"), clamping to the ceiling.
// The quantity that must be bounded is the process's resident BYTES against its envelope; the
// entry count is only a proxy for it, and the proxy's calibration is the one number that cannot be
// observed before resolving (the bootstrap above) — so it silently under-counted by ~6.7x on the
// 2026-11-12 PXE 2c lane (measured ~20 MiB per entry) and ran the working set past the 24 GiB
// envelope until the governor refused it as `MemoryStallRefusedPageThrash`. The stall-refusal
// module already states why a count cannot catch it: "the thrash is at PAGE grain underneath the
// allocator, invisible to entry counts" (gunbc.host.memory_stall_refusal). So the count-from-a-
// constant is the unjustified link, and repairing it by re-hand-picking the constant (20 MiB)
// would put the very same knob back at the measured link, stale with the next tree.
//
// Repair, at that boundary, and only ever LOWERING memory (operator ruling: budgets are lowered,
// never raised): the entry-count cap stays as a NON-authoritative backstop (the estimate below is
// left untouched, so no new footprint estimate is adopted), and the OBSERVED budget the cap is
// already denominated in becomes a live bound on the resident set. When this process's own resident
// footprint reaches `typed_cache_pressure_high_water_basis_points` of the observed budget, the
// cache YIELDS entries until resident recedes to `typed_cache_pressure_low_water_basis_points` (a
// hysteretic band, not a drain to `typed_module_cache_entries_floor` -- see below) so the run gets
// its memory back for its own correctness work, instead of the kernel evicting the run's own pages.
// The authority
// is the host/slot memory envelope (gunbc.host.memory_envelope, gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired
// memory_high/memory_max, read through gunbc.host.host_budget_source) — NOT a harness-local number.
//
// What is sampled WHEN (precise, not "sample the whole thing once"): the OBSERVED budget is a
// run-start fact, memoized once (the run-start discipline that keeps the 2026-07-21 host-noise
// treadmill out). The resident footprint is read LIVE once per cache insert — this arm is live per
// insert on purpose, because it bounds a quantity (accumulated cache bytes) that only grows as the
// run proceeds; it is NOT a re-derivation of the cap or budget (those never move after the first
// sample), so it is not the per-insert feedback loop that OOM'd in 2026.
//
// `typed_cache_pressure_high_water_basis_points` is POLICY, not a derived fact about the workload:
// it is a safety margin under the hard ceiling. Its reason — from a receipt, not a law: on the
// 2026-11-12 PXE lane a completing run peaked at 16.6 GiB of a 24 GiB budget (~69%), so any line
// strictly above that observed clean peak and strictly below 100% (the ceiling the thrashing run
// reached) bounds the thrashing run identically while leaving a completing run untouched. 8750 bp
// leaves ~3 GiB under the ceiling to shed one module's transient allocation. It makes no claim
// about per-entry size, so it does not go stale with the next tree (unlike the estimate). It is
// applied only to an OBSERVED budget (HostBudgetResolved) — a declared planning ceiling, an
// externally-held bound, or an unreadable cgroup leaves the arm inert (host_budget_source forbids
// promoting a declared ceiling into an enforced limit; a bound that cannot observe must not
// fabricate the zero that would flush the whole cache).
data typed_cache_pressure_high_water_basis_points: BasisPoint = basis_point(count: 8750)

// Is this process's resident footprint at or above the pressure line of the observed budget?
// Absence is kept as absence (`ByteSize?`, mirroring the Rust `Option<u64>`), never coerced to 0:
// `none` (ExternallyBounded / Unreadable / a declared-only ceiling that was not promoted) and a
// degenerate `Present(0)` are both inert — a bound that cannot observe the envelope must not act,
// and must not fabricate the zero (resident >= 0 is always true) that would flush the whole cache.
// Consumer: the executing witnesses resident_pressure_inclusive_at_line,
// resident_pressure_absent_or_zero_budget_is_inert and resident_pressure_leaves_a_completing_run_untouched
// (test.claim.typed_module_cache_capacity_witness) run this fold over the same cases as the Rust
// tests in memory_governor; the Rust executor is cli_run::enforce_typed_cache_entry_cap via
// memory_governor::resident_exceeds_cache_pressure_line.
fn resident_exceeds_cache_pressure_line(resident_bytes: ByteSize, budget_bytes: ByteSize?) -> Bool {
  match budget_bytes {
    none => false
    Present { value: b } => {
      let budget_count = byte_size_count(b: b)
      if budget_count == 0 {
        false
      } else {
        byte_size_count(b: resident_bytes)
          >= budget_count * basis_point_count(bp: typed_cache_pressure_high_water_basis_points) / 10000
      }
    }
  }
}

// The release line, below the trigger, giving the pressure arm HYSTERESIS. Once the trigger fires
// the controller evicts UNTIL resident recedes here, then lets the cache refill. Releasing BELOW the
// trigger (not at it) leaves a band so the next insert -- reading the same now-fallen resident --
// does not immediately re-trigger; the cache settles just under the trigger instead of draining to
// the floor on every later insert, which would make it a 100-entry FIFO manufacturing readmission
// churn. The floor stays only as the hard stop when even a full yield cannot relieve pressure, and
// there the page-grain stall governor refuses on its own authority.
//
// A POLICY margin of the same kind as the trigger, not a workload fact: 8000 bp is a settle point
// set well below the observed clean peak (~69%) so a run that never reached the trigger is left
// fully alone. Eviction only lowers the resident set if the freed heap is returned to the allocator
// -- glibc keeps freed arenas resident -- so the Rust executor trims (malloc_trim) after each yield
// batch and re-reads; measured 2026-11-12, dropping the cache WITHOUT a trim leaves VmRSS unchanged,
// WITH a trim it collapses. The release test is therefore meaningful only beside the trim.
data typed_cache_pressure_low_water_basis_points: BasisPoint = basis_point(count: 8000)

// Has this process's resident footprint receded to or below the release line? Absence kept as
// absence, never coerced to 0 (see resident_exceeds_cache_pressure_line): `none` and `Present(0)`
// are both "not receded", so the controller never drains to the floor on a missing budget. Consumer:
// the executing witness resident_pressure_releases_below_low_water, and the Rust executor
// cli_run::enforce_typed_cache_entry_cap via
// memory_governor::resident_recedes_below_cache_pressure_line.
fn resident_recedes_below_cache_pressure_line(resident_bytes: ByteSize, budget_bytes: ByteSize?) -> Bool {
  match budget_bytes {
    none => false
    Present { value: b } => {
      let budget_count = byte_size_count(b: b)
      if budget_count == 0 {
        false
      } else {
        byte_size_count(b: resident_bytes)
          <= budget_count * basis_point_count(bp: typed_cache_pressure_low_water_basis_points) / 10000
      }
    }
  }
}


// Derived bound vs refusal stay disjoint: only CapEntries may bound a resolve from
// observed or declared bytes. An externally bounded executor is not a derived bound
// and must not reuse CapEntries (review 80724). CapEntriesExternallyBounded names
// that standing; the floor is the working-set size under that standing, not a
// fabricated host RSS. It dissolves when the executor exposes a readable memory.max.
data typed_module_cache_externally_bounded_dissolves_on: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(
  description: "the BuildBuddy (or other ExternallyBoundedExecutor) namespace exposes a readable cgroup memory.max or memory.high, so typed_module_cache_entry_cap derives CapEntries from HostBudgetResolved and CapEntriesExternallyBounded deletes"
)

type TypedModuleCacheCap
  = CapEntries { entries: Int }
  | CapEntryRefused { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | CapEntriesExternallyBounded { entries: Int, run_id: NonEmptyStr }

// The derivation the seed hand-realizes. Division by the per-entry estimate,
// then clamped into the declared band -- the clamp is what keeps a very small
// budget from producing a cap of zero (which would thrash) and a very large one
// from producing an unbounded working set.
// A declared-unverified request is an ENTRY-COUNT planning input, not an RSS cap. Keeping the
// request usable here preserves remote executors whose enforced limit is outside the container
// namespace without promoting the declaration into an observed HostBudgetResolved.
fn typed_module_cache_entry_cap(r: HostBudgetResolution) -> TypedModuleCacheCap {
  match r {
    HostBudgetResolved { effective_bytes: b, requested_bytes: _, observation: _ } =>
      CapEntries { entries: clamp_to_declared_band(raw: raw_entry_cap(budget: b)) }
    HostBudgetDeclaredUnverified { requested_bytes: requested, reason: _ } =>
      CapEntries { entries: clamp_to_declared_band(raw: raw_entry_cap(budget: requested)) }
    HostBudgetUnreadable { reason: why } => CapEntryRefused { reason: why }
    HostBudgetExternallyBounded { run_id: id } =>
      CapEntriesExternallyBounded { entries: typed_module_cache_entries_floor, run_id: id }
  }
}

fn raw_entry_cap(budget: ByteSize) -> Int {
  byte_size_count(b: budget) / byte_size_count(b: typed_module_bytes_per_entry_estimate)
}

fn clamp_to_declared_band(raw: Int) -> Int {
  int_min(
    a: int_max(a: raw, b: typed_module_cache_entries_floor),
    b: typed_module_cache_entries_ceiling
  )
}

// The operator/test probe the seed honours: a positive override is accepted but
// still clamped to the ceiling, so the probe can never widen past the modelled
// band. A zero or negative override is not an override at all -- it falls
// through to the derived cap rather than being treated as "no limit".
fn typed_module_cache_entry_cap_with_override(
  override_entries: Int,
  r: HostBudgetResolution
) -> TypedModuleCacheCap {
  if override_entries > 0 {
    CapEntries { entries: int_min(a: override_entries, b: typed_module_cache_entries_ceiling) }
  } else {
    typed_module_cache_entry_cap(r: r)
  }
}

fn typed_module_cache_cap_is_refused(c: TypedModuleCacheCap) -> Bool {
  match c {
    CapEntries { entries: _ } => false
    CapEntryRefused { reason: _ } => true
    CapEntriesExternallyBounded { entries: _, run_id: _ } => false
  }
}

data typed_module_cache_externally_bounded_disposition: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity",
    decl_name: "CapEntriesExternallyBounded",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

fn cap_entries_externally_bounded_still_interim() -> Bool {
  match dissolution_status(
    condition: typed_module_cache_externally_bounded_dissolves_on,
    present_decls: [],
  ) {
    DissolutionUnbound => true
    DissolutionPending => false
    DissolutionFired => false
  }
}

data typed_module_cache_capacity_seed_mirror_disposition: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity",
    decl_name: "typed_module_cache_entry_cap",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

data typed_module_cache_capacity_seed_mirror_note: String = "v1_compiler.cli_run typed_module_cache_cap_derivation IS THIS DERIVATION, hand-realized in the seed, and it is declared here rather than left implicit. It composes the same division, the same floor/ceiling clamp, the same positive-override rule, and the same refusal -- and nothing executes a check that the two agree, which is exactly the drift this repository has already paid for elsewhere.\n\nWhy it cannot be deleted today: the cap bounds the memory used to RESOLVE the corpus, so its consumer runs strictly before any .dag value could exist. The seed needs the value baked in. That is a bootstrap constraint, not ordinary seed-shrink debt, and conflating the two would misprice the work.\n\nTHE INTERIM LENS NOW EXISTS AND EXECUTES: test.claim.seed_mirror_constant_lens_witness joins all five in-scope seed constants to their authority rows, including this module's three. It derives each expected literal from the row rather than writing it down, so it cannot be satisfied by editing the lens, and it REFUSES an uncited or unparseable constant rather than skipping it. This paragraph previously described that lens in the future tense while nothing executed, and a dissolution trigger naming a mechanism that does not run reads as guarded to every later author -- which is measured, not hypothetical: DECLARED_RUNNER_SLOT_MEMORY_HIGH_BYTES sat eleven days at a value its authority had superseded, under exactly this sentence.

WHAT REMAINS UNDISSOLVED, which is the larger half: the lens checks that the mirror AGREES, not that the mirror is unnecessary. The full dissolution is still the emit path baking this module's constants into the seed so cli_run stops composing its own arithmetic and reads them. Until that lands the AGREEMENT half of this class remains MITIGATABLE, and the 2026-08-20 correction below is to the REASON rather than to the rung.\n\nWHAT THIS SENTENCE SAID AND WHY IT CHANGED: it read that the lens declares ReadsLiveTree because it reads .rs text, that the required floor folds one hermetic subject, and that the lens is therefore DISCOVERED AND DECLINED with nothing executing it automatically. The measurement in it is preserved and still correct -- adding the lens moved the floor's declined_live count by exactly its four witnesses and its claims count by zero (gunbc#8638, run 32337765205 against 32333231183 at the same base). The INFERENCE from it was wrong. A hermetic Filesystem.Read of a committed checkout path is input access rather than a host effect (v1_interpreter's checkout-read carve-out), so reading committed .rs text never actually required a non-hermetic route; what declines the lens is a separate file-grain prediction in the floor's site projection, which is measured as removable (floor run 32345970386: deleted on a branch, ~783 identities admitted, 626 pass) but is NOT yet removed on main, because the same deletion admits 55 blockers staged behind their own owners. So the rung stays where it was and the path to climbing it is now known and cheap rather than unknown.\n\nTHE UNDISSOLVED HALF IS UNCHANGED AND IS STILL THE LARGER ONE: the lens checks that the mirror AGREES, not that the mirror is unnecessary, and the full dissolution remains the emit path baking this module's constants into the seed so cli_run stops composing its own arithmetic and reads them. test.claim.seed_mirror_constant_lens_witness seed_mirror_lens_floor_execution_note carries the measurement, the correction, and the surviving residual (an unmarked seed constant is invisible to both arms)."
